{
  "id": "08-discern-and-carry",
  "title": "Discern and Carry",
  "tenet": "Test the prompting; act in love",
  "durationMs": 76728,
  "audioAsset": "kingdom/08-discern-and-carry.mp3",
  "transcriptAsset": "assets/kingdom/08-discern-and-carry.json",
  "text": "What if the first thought after prayer is gentle, inconvenient, and easy to edit?\n\nA man prayed about a quarrel with his friend. The first thought that came was simple: call him, listen, and apologize for your part. A quiet sense of rightness accompanied it. Then another thought arrived: wait until he admits everything, and tell others how patient you have been. The man wrote down the first thought before his pride could dress it in better clothes. He prayed again, weighed it against Christ's call to truth and love, and made the call. His friend did not say everything he hoped to hear. Still, the conversation began to heal.\n\nOften the first thought after sincere prayer arrives before self protection has time to edit it. Attend to it, especially when it brings a steady peace and asks for love rather than applause. That feeling is a signal to discern, not proof that every thought comes from God. Bias can enter as we interpret or carry out an insight. Ask: would I hear the same thing if it cost me comfort or credit? Confirm it in prayer. Test it with Scripture and wise counsel, especially when a decision could seriously affect someone. Then do not let endless hesitation replace a safe, faithful act. Carry the good without letting ego rewrite it.",
